 why error

    Query successful

The error occurs because Julia treats the backslash (\) inside a string as an escape character (e.g., \n for a new line or \t for a tab).

In the path provided, \F and \j are not valid escape sequences, which triggers the ERROR: syntax: invalid escape sequence.
How to Fix It

Gemini suggests three ways to resolve this in Julia:

1. Use Forward Slashes (Recommended) Even on Windows, Julia (and most modern programming languages) prefers forward slashes for file paths. This avoids the escape character issue entirely.
Julia

include("Fugacity Coefficients/FugacityCo.jl")

2. Use Double Backslashes If a backslash must be used, it must be "escaped" with another backslash.
Julia

include("Fugacity Coefficients\\FugacityCo.jl")

3. Use Raw Strings By placing an r before the opening quote, Julia is told to treat the string "raw" and ignore escape sequences.
Julia

include(raw"Fugacity Coefficients\FugacityCo.jl")

A Note on File Paths

The screenshot shows a space in the folder name Fugacity Coefficients. While the fixes above handle the syntax error, always ensure that the Julia REPL's current working directory is the parent folder of "Fugacity Coefficients".

You can check the current directory by typing pwd() in the Julia prompt.

