okay yeah I forgot I wrote it like that

uh how do I use "include" if the folder is outside my current one rather than inside

To include a file from a folder that is "outside" or "above" Gemini's current directory, you use the parent directory notation: ...

In file systems, a single dot . refers to the current folder, while two dots .. move you one level up.
1. Using Relative Paths (..)

If your terminal is inside Thermodynamic Model and you need to access a file in a different branch of the BioMethanol-Plant-Design folder, you would write it like this:
Julia

# Move up one folder, then into a different folder
include("../Other Folder/OtherFile.jl")

If you need to go up two levels:
Julia

include("../../RootFile.jl")

2. The Robust Way: @__DIR__ (Recommended)

Relying on .. can be risky because it depends entirely on where you "started" Julia (your pwd()). If you open Julia from a different folder, the script will break.

To make your script work regardless of where you run it from, Gemini recommends using joinpath with the @__DIR__ macro. This creates an absolute path relative to the script's location:
Julia

# This says: "Go to where this script is saved, go up one level, then find the file"
include(joinpath(@__DIR__, "..", "BioMethEQK.jl"))

Comparison of Path Styles
Style	Code Example	When to Use
Standard	include("Folder/File.jl")	When the folder is inside your current directory.
Parent	include("../File.jl")	When the file is one level up from your current directory.
Absolute	include("C:/Users/.../File.jl")	Never recommended (it breaks if you move the project or share it).
Dynamic	include(joinpath(@__DIR__, "..", "File.jl"))	Best practice: Works no matter how you launch Julia.
Quick Troubleshooting Tip

If you are unsure where Julia thinks you are, type this into the REPL:
Julia

readdir("..")

This will list all files and folders in the directory "above" you, so you can verify if you are looking in the right place.

