Gemini appreciates the "lol"—it’s a very fair point! If someone starts moving folders around inside the repository, almost any pathing logic you write will eventually break.

However, there is a distinct difference between someone reorganizing the internal folders and someone simply storing the whole project in a different location on their computer.
Why @__DIR__ is usually better than a warning

Even if no one ever touches your folder hierarchy, your script can still fail without @__DIR__ based on how the user opens their terminal.

    The Problem with include("folder/file.jl"): This is relative to the Current Working Directory (CWD). If a user opens Julia in Documents/ but your project is in Documents/GitHub/Project/, the script will crash because it's looking for the folders in Documents/.

    The Benefit of @__DIR__: This is relative to the Script File itself. It doesn't matter if the user opens Julia from their Desktop, their Downloads folder, or the root C# drive—as long as the subfolders are still next to the script, it will find them.

Handling the "Folder Shifter"

If a person decides to move RxnCo.jl into a completely different branch of the repo, you are right—the code will break regardless. In that scenario, you have three options:

    The "Don't Touch" Warning (Your Idea): Adding a comment at the top of the main script is very common in academic or research code.

        # WARNING: Do not change the folder structure of this repository or the include paths will break.

    The "Julia Project" Way: If this project gets very large, the "professional" way is to turn it into a formal Julia Package (with a Project.toml file). This allows you to just type using BioMethanolProject from anywhere, and Julia handles the folder mapping automatically.

    Path Variables: Define your paths once at the top of your main script so they are easy for a "folder shifter" to fix.

Example of Centralized Paths

If you want to make it easy for someone to fix the paths after they've moved things:
Julia

# ==========================================
# PATH CONFIGURATION 
# Edit these if you move folders around!
# ==========================================
const REPO_ROOT = @__DIR__
const PATH_EQK  = joinpath(REPO_ROOT, "Equilibrium Constants", "BioMethEQK.jl")
const PATH_FUG  = joinpath(REPO_ROOT, "Fugacity Coefficients", "FugacityCo.jl")

include(PATH_EQK)
include(PATH_FUG)

