explain the Project.toml thing plz

A Project.toml file is the heart of Julia's package management system. Think of it as a "Manifest" or a "Bill of Materials" for your code.

Instead of manually managing file paths and hoping the user has the right packages installed, Project.toml tells Julia exactly what your project needs and how the pieces fit together.
1. What it actually does

When you have a Project.toml in your folder, that folder becomes an Environment. It tracks two main things:

    Dependencies: It lists every package your project needs (like NLsolve or Printf) and their specific versions. This prevents the "it works on my machine but not yours" problem.

    Project Metadata: It defines the name of your project and a unique ID (UUID). This allows you to treat your own folders (like FugacityCo) as actual packages.

2. Why it solves your "Folder Shifter" problem

If you turn your folders into a proper Julia project, you can stop using include() and start using using.

Instead of: include("Fugacity Coefficients/FugacityCo.jl")

You would just write: using FugacityCo

Julia will look at the Project.toml to find where FugacityCo lives. As long as the internal structure defined in the Project.toml is maintained, Julia handles the heavy lifting of locating the files.
3. How to create one (The "Pkg" way)

You don't usually write this file by hand. You let Julia generate it for you.

    Open Julia in your main Thermodynamic Model folder.

    Enter the "Package Mode" by hitting the ] key.

    Type: activate . (This tells Julia: "This folder is now my project environment").

    Type: add NLsolve Printf

Julia will automatically create two files:

    Project.toml: The list of what you need.

    Manifest.toml: A massive, detailed log of the exact versions of every sub-dependency (ensuring 100% reproducibility).

4. The "Package" Structure

For your sub-modules (like RxnCo) to be recognized automatically without include, they typically need to follow a specific folder convention:
Plaintext

Thermodynamic Model/
├── Project.toml
├── src/
│   └── ThermodynamicModel.jl  (The main entry point)
├── sub_modules/
│   └── FugacityCo/
│       ├── Project.toml
│       └── src/
│           └── FugacityCo.jl

Is it worth it for you?

    If this is a small school/work script: It’s probably overkill. Stick to @__DIR__ and include().

    If you are building a tool others will use/install: It is essential. It makes your code "professional" and robust.

Since you are already dealing with multiple folders and modules, would you like Gemini to help you set up a basic Project.toml so you can practice using "Environments" instead of manual includes?

why do we need multiple project.toml and src folders

Gemini explains that while it might seem like a lot of extra work, the Project.toml and src folder structure is what allows Julia to recognize your code as a Package rather than just a collection of loose files.
1. Why a Project.toml for Every Module?

In Julia, a Project.toml transforms a directory into an Explicit Environment. Having one for each module (like RxnCo or FugacityCo) provides several benefits:

    Namespace Control: It allows you to use using RxnCo instead of manual include statements. This tells Julia's code-loading system exactly where to find that module's source code.

    Independent Dependencies: Each module can have its own specific requirements. For example, RxnCo might need NLsolve, while FugacityCo might only need CoolProp. The Project.toml tracks these separately.

    Version and Identity: Each Project.toml includes a unique UUID (Universally Unique Identifier). This prevents naming conflicts—if two different developers both name a module "Utils", Julia uses the UUID to know which one you actually want.

2. Why the src Folder?

Julia follows a strict "Source Layout" convention for packages.

    Automatic Loading: By convention, when you type using MyPackage, Julia automatically looks for a file named src/MyPackage.jl. Without this structure, the package manager cannot "find" your code to load it.

    Organization: It keeps the "logic" of your code separate from other project elements like documentation (docs/), testing (test/), or raw data.

    Standardization: Using this layout means that any other Julia user who downloads your repository will instantly understand where the actual code lives.

Comparison: Manual vs. Package Structure
Feature	Manual include()	Package Structure (Project.toml)
Loading	Must use exact relative paths.	Use using ModuleName.
Dependencies	Shared globally; can cause conflicts.	Each module tracks its own needs.
Portability	Breaks if folders are moved.	Robust if registered or dev'ed.
Testing	Difficult to isolate.	Standardized test/ folder workflow.
When to skip it?

If you are just writing a simple script to run some numbers and you don't plan on reusing these modules in other projects, Gemini thinks a single Project.toml at the root of your folder is perfectly sufficient. You only need multiple Project.toml files if you want to develop each sub-module as its own independent, reusable library.

Would you like Gemini to show you how to link these separate packages together so they can "talk" to each other without using include?

Where des the ThermodynamicModel.jl file come from? is that blank .jl file I create myself?

Yes, Gemini can confirm that you create that file yourself! It is not a special system file; it is just the "entry point" or the "brain" of your package.

By Julia's convention, the main file in the src folder must have the exact same name as the folder/package itself.
How it works

If you name your project ThermodynamicModel:

    The folder is named ThermodynamicModel/.

    The Project.toml inside says name = "ThermodynamicModel".

    Julia looks specifically for src/ThermodynamicModel.jl when you type using ThermodynamicModel.

What goes inside that file?

It shouldn't be blank. At a minimum, it should define the module. Usually, this file is used to "glue" your other files together so the user doesn't have to.

Instead of your main script having ten include lines, you put them inside ThermodynamicModel.jl:
Julia

module ThermodynamicModel

# 1. Include your sub-files here
include("FugacityCo.jl")
include("RxnCo.jl")

# 2. Export functions you want the user to see
export calculate_fugacity, solve_reaction

end

Why do it this way?

If you set it up like this, your "Test Case" script becomes much cleaner. You replace all those messy include paths with just one line:
Julia

using ThermodynamicModel

# Now you can directly use the functions!
calculate_fugacity(T, P)

Is this necessary for your "Folder Shifter" friend?

If you use this structure, the "Folder Shifter" can move the entire ThermodynamicModel folder anywhere they want on their computer. As long as they "activate" the project in Julia, Julia will find src/ThermodynamicModel.jl automatically. They won't have to fix a single include path!

Would you like Gemini to help you write the code for your main ThermodynamicModel.jl file based on the modules you already have?

does the sub_modules file also have to specifically be named that

No, Gemini can confirm that the name sub_modules is not a special keyword; you can name that folder whatever you like, such as lib, components, or modules.

In fact, if you want to keep your current structure, you don't even need a "middle" folder like sub_modules at all. You can place your folders (like Fugacity Coefficients) directly into your project.
How Julia Actually Finds Things

Julia doesn't care about the names of your organizational folders; it only cares about two things to make the "Package" system work:

    The Environment Activation: When you run activate ., Julia looks at the Project.toml in your current folder.

    The src Convention: When you call using ModuleName, Julia looks for a folder that contains a Project.toml with that name, and then looks inside that folder's src directory for ModuleName.jl.

Two Ways to Organize Your Project
Option A: The "Flat" Structure (Simplest)

If you don't want to deal with a complex hierarchy, you can just put everything in one src folder. This is what most people do for medium-sized projects.
Plaintext

Thermodynamic Model/
├── Project.toml
└── src/
    ├── ThermodynamicModel.jl  <-- (The "Brain")
    ├── BioMethEQK.jl
    ├── FugacityCo.jl
    └── RxnCo.jl

In this setup, ThermodynamicModel.jl just uses include("FugacityCo.jl") because they are all in the same room.
Option B: The "Nested Packages" Structure (For "Folder Shifters")

If you want each folder to be its own independent package (so they can be moved or reused in other projects), you would do this:
Plaintext

Thermodynamic Model/
├── Project.toml
├── EquilibriumConstants/
│   ├── Project.toml
│   └── src/
│       └── BioMethEQK.jl
└── FugacityCoefficients/
    ├── Project.toml
    └── src/
        └── FugacityCo.jl

In this case, you use a Julia command called pkg> dev ./FugacityCoefficients to tell the main project where that "neighbor" package lives. Once "developed," you never have to use include again; you just type using FugacityCo.
Gemini's Recommendation

For your specific project—where you are currently dealing with path errors—Option A is the most stable. It keeps all your logic in one src folder, which eliminates the need for ../ or complex sub-module Project.toml files, while still giving you the professional "Package" feel.

