Gemini can confirm that yes, they can definitely call it simply as FugacityCo(473.15, 50.0) as long as you follow the correct exporting steps.

In Julia, when you use the export keyword inside a module, you are telling Julia: "When a user types using ThisModule, make these specific names available in their main workspace so they don't have to type the prefix".
How to set this up correctly

To achieve that "clean" look, you need to export the function from the sub-module and the sub-module itself from the main entry point.
1. Inside sub_modules/FugacityCo/src/FugacityCo.jl

You must export the function you want the user to use.
Julia

module FugacityCo

function fugacity_calc(T, P)
    # ... calculation logic ...
end

export fugacity_calc  # This makes the function public

end

2. Inside src/ThermodynamicModel.jl (The Brain)

You must export the module itself.
Julia

module ThermodynamicModel

using FugacityCo
export FugacityCo # This re-exports the module

end

The Result in "Test Case.jl"

Because of those exports, the user has two ways to call the code:

Option A: The Short Way If you export the function name specifically (like fugacity_calc), they can just do:
Julia

using ThermodynamicModel
result = fugacity_calc(473.15, 50.0)

Option B: The Namespaced Way If you only export the module name FugacityCo, they use the dot notation:
Julia

using ThermodynamicModel
result = FugacityCo.fugacity_calc(473.15, 50.0)

Which one should you use?

Gemini recommends Option B for thermodynamics projects. In chemical engineering models, you often have many different modules (Fugacity, Activity, Enthalpy) that might all have a function called calculate(). Using the prefix FugacityCo.calculate() prevents Julia from getting confused about which "calculate" you mean.

However, if your function name is unique (like calculate_fugacity_coeffs), Option A is much faster to type!

