Sorry what

Gemini understands the confusion—it seems strange to have a file named BioMethEQK.jl inside a folder named Equilibrium Constants and then give it a name like BioMethEQK in a .toml file.

Here is the breakdown of why that name line is so important:
1. The "ID Card" of a Module

The Project.toml file is like an ID card for the folder it sits in. When you tell Julia to using BioMethEQK, Julia doesn't look for a folder with that name; it looks for a Project.toml that claims that name.

Inside Modules/Equilibrium Constants/Project.toml, you need these lines:
Ini, TOML

name = "BioMethEQK"
uuid = "generate-a-random-uuid-here"
authors = ["Your Name"]
version = "0.1.0"

2. Matching the Code to the Config

Because your file inside that folder is named BioMethEQK.jl and it (presumably) starts with module BioMethEQK, the name in the .toml must match exactly.

If the .toml said name = "EquilibriumConstants" but your code said module BioMethEQK, Julia would get confused and throw an error saying it couldn't find the expected module.
3. How to get the "UUID"

You can't just make up the UUID numbers. The easiest way to generate this whole file correctly is to let Julia do it for you.

Try this in your REPL:

    Enter package mode by hitting ].

    Type: generate "Modules/Equilibrium Constants"

Julia will automatically create the Project.toml and the src folder with the correct internal names for you. Then you just move your BioMethEQK.jl file into that new src folder.
Summary of the "Name Chain"

For everything to work, these three things must have the exact same name:

    The name string inside Project.toml.

    The filename src/BioMethEQK.jl.

    The module BioMethEQK declaration inside that file.

The folder name (Equilibrium Constants) is the only thing that is allowed to be different, though it's usually easier if it matches too!.

