using FugacityCo

# Your math logic stays the same, but now it uses the modules correctly
function rxnco(T, P, phi, guess, feed_co2, feed_h2)
    # This can now call BioMethEQK.calculate_k() or similar
    # ...
end

export rxnco

end

Step 3: Switch Back to Main

Once you’ve linked the sub-modules, remember to switch your REPL back to your main project so you can run your test case:
Julia

Pkg.activate(".")

Why this is better than include

    No Redundant Loading: If you use include in three different files, Julia might try to define the same module three times, causing "Replace Module" warnings or errors.

    Precompilation: Julia will precompile these modules as proper packages, making your main loop run faster after the first execution.

    Independence: RxnCo now explicitly states "I need these two modules to function," which makes the code much easier to debug.

