okay so once the modules that I'm exporting also export the function, that means the user can call that specific function just by using ThermodynamicModel right

Exactly! If you have everything set up this way, the user only needs to call using ThermodynamicModel to access the functions from all the sub-modules.

However, there is one technical nuance in Julia regarding Re-exporting. Just because ThermodynamicModel uses BioMethEQK, and BioMethEQK exports calculate_k, it doesn't automatically mean calculate_k is available to someone who only types using ThermodynamicModel.
How to ensure the functions are available

Gemini suggests two ways to handle this in your "brain" file (ThermodynamicModel.jl):
1. The Manual Export (Most Control)

If you want the user to call calculate_k() directly, you must explicitly export those names from your main module as well.
Julia

module ThermodynamicModel

using BioMethEQK
using FugacityCo

# Export the module names (user must type FugacityCo.function_name)
export BioMethEQK, FugacityCo

# Export specific functions (user can just type function_name)
export calculate_k, fugacity_calc 

end

2. The "Reexport" Package (Easiest)

There is a popular Julia package called Reexport.jl designed specifically for this situation. It "bubbles up" all exports from a sub-module to the main module automatically.

If you add Reexport to your project, your file looks like this:
Julia

module ThermodynamicModel

using Reexport

@reexport using BioMethEQK
@reexport using FugacityCo
# ... etc

end

Why this is great for your project

With this setup, your Test Case.jl becomes incredibly clean. You can treat the entire folder structure as a single scientific library.

Your script would look like this:
Julia

using ThermodynamicModel

# These functions are now available directly because 
# they were exported by the sub-modules and re-exported by the main one!
k_val = calculate_k(T)
phi = fugacity_calc(T, P)

