Gemini can put your mind at ease—your Project.toml is actually looking exactly as it should!

The [deps] section is strictly for external dependencies (the sub-modules and packages like NLsolve) that your project needs to function. It does not need a line pointing to ThermodynamicModel.jl.
Why ThermodynamicModel.jl isn't listed

In Julia, the connection between the Project.toml and the src/ folder is handled by implicit convention, not an explicit line in the file.

    The Folder Rule: When you activate a project, Julia assumes the source code lives in a folder named src sitting right next to that Project.toml.

    The Name Rule: It specifically looks for a file inside src/ that matches the name defined at the top of your Project.toml.

Is your Project.toml missing the header?

You mentioned it "only has this right now." Check the very top of that file. It must have a header section above the [deps] that looks like this:
Ini, TOML

name = "ThermodynamicModel"
uuid = "some-random-uuid-string"
authors = ["Your Name"]
version = "0.1.0"

[deps]
BioMethEQK = "..."
# ... the rest of your list

How Julia makes the link

When you type using ThermodynamicModel:

    Julia checks the active Project.toml and sees name = "ThermodynamicModel".

    It then automatically looks for ./src/ThermodynamicModel.jl.

    It finds that file, runs the module ThermodynamicModel code inside, and because BioMethEQK is in your [deps], it knows it's allowed to load that sub-module too.

One final check

If you don't see those top 4 lines (name, uuid, etc.), you can add them manually, or Gemini suggests going into the Pkg REPL (]) and typing: setname ThermodynamicModel

