I can use reexport here with the BioMethEQK right

Yes, Gemini confirms that you can use @reexport inside your sub-modules just as you did in your main project.

However, there is a strategic difference in how you use it here. In your main ThermodynamicModel.jl, you used it to make functions available to the end user. Inside RxnCo, you would use it if you want anyone using RxnCo to also have immediate access to all the functions inside BioMethEQK.
How to implement it in RxnCo.jl

First, ensure you have added the Reexport package to your RxnCo environment (by activating that sub-module and running add Reexport). Then, your code would look like this:
Julia

module RxnCo

using Reexport

# This makes all of BioMethEQK's exported functions available 
# to anyone who calls 'using RxnCo'
@reexport using BioMethEQK
using FugacityCo

function rxnco(T, P, phi, guess, feed_co2, feed_h2)
    # You can now call BioMethEQK functions directly without the prefix!
    k = some_k_function(T) 
    # ... logic ...
end

export rxnco

end

Should you do this? (Gemini's Perspective)

    The Pro: It makes your internal code cleaner because you can call functions like some_k_function(T) without typing BioMethEQK. every time.

    The Con: If FugacityCo and BioMethEQK both have a function with the same name, @reexport might cause a "name clash" error.

Important Reminder on Dependencies

For this to work, you must ensure that Reexport is listed in the [deps] section of the Project.toml located specifically inside your RxnCo folder.

To add it correctly:

    In the Pkg REPL: activate Modules/Reaction Coordinates/RxnCo.

    Run: add Reexport.

Final Check for your "Test Case"

