also, since we established at the very beginning that NLsolve and Printf are gonna be used, I can omit any individual instances of these in the sub modules right

Actually, Gemini must clarify that you cannot omit them from the sub-modules if those sub-modules use them.

In Julia's package system, each module is an independent "island". Even if your main project has NLsolve and Printf, a sub-module like RxnCo cannot "see" them unless they are explicitly added to that sub-module's own Project.toml.
Why you need to include them individually

If RxnCo uses the @printf macro or the nlsolve() function, it needs its own "permission" to use those packages.

    Isolated Environments: Each Project.toml defines exactly what that specific module needs to run.

    Precompilation: When Julia precompiles RxnCo, it looks at RxnCo's Project.toml. If NLsolve isn't there, the precompilation will fail because it doesn't know what nlsolve is.

    Portability: If you ever wanted to share just the RxnCo module with a colleague, it wouldn't work for them if it relied on your main project's Project.toml to find its dependencies.

How to do it quickly

You don't need to download the packages again, but you do need to register them in each sub-module that uses them.

For your RxnCo module:

    Enter Pkg mode (]).

    activate Modules/Reaction Coordinates/RxnCo.

    add NLsolve Printf.

Does this mean you have multiple copies?

No! Julia's package manager is smart. It only keeps one copy of NLsolve on your hard drive, but it creates a reference to it in every Project.toml that needs it.
The Rule of Thumb

    If a file has using NLsolve or using Printf at the top, that package must be in that folder's Project.toml.

    If a sub-module (like FugacityCo) only does pure math and doesn't use Printf or NLsolve, then you can omit them there.

