Would you like Gemini to show you how to check which environment is currently active while you are running your code?

if I include it and someone runs it in an already active environment, will it cause issues?

Gemini can reassure you that including Pkg.activate(".") in your script will not cause issues, even if an environment is already active.

Here is why it is safe and actually beneficial:
1. It is Idempotent

If the user has already activated the correct environment (the one in that folder), calling Pkg.activate(".") again does nothing more than confirm that same environment is active. It won't restart the session or clear your variables.
2. It Overrides the "Global" Environment

If a user has a different environment active (like their default global one), this line will switch Julia over to your project's Project.toml. This is exactly what you want, as it ensures the script uses the specific versions of NLsolve and your sub-modules that you have tested.
3. It Prevents "Missing Package" Errors

The most common frustration for people running shared code is getting an error saying a package isn't found. By including Pkg.activate("."), you are taking the guesswork out of it for them by pointing Julia directly to your dependency list.
The Workflow Gemini Recommends

Keep the lines exactly like this at the very top of Test Case.jl:
Julia

import Pkg
Pkg.activate(".") # Sets the context to your Project.toml

using ThermodynamicModel # Loads your logic
using NLsolve, Printf    # Loads external tools

What happens if they run it twice?

If you or your friend run the script multiple times in the same session (e.g., using include("Test Case.jl")), Julia will simply see that the environment is already active and proceed to the using statements. Julia is smart enough to know that if ThermodynamicModel is already loaded, it doesn't need to re-load everything from scratch, which saves time.

