Couldn't I have used the full path

Gemini can confirm that yes, you absolutely could have used the full (absolute) path.

However, there is a very specific reason why Julia users typically avoid full paths, especially when working on a project meant for a "Folder Shifter" friend.
The Problem with Full Paths

If you use a full path like C:\Users\YourName\Documents\Thermodynamic Model\Modules\..., Julia writes that exact string into your Manifest.toml.

    On Your Computer: It works perfectly.

    On Your Friend's Computer: It will immediately break. When your friend tries to run the code, Julia will look for C:\Users\YourName\..., which doesn't exist on their machine. They would have to manually re-develop every single link themselves.

The Benefit of Relative Paths (../)

By using ../../Equilibrium Constants/BioMethEQK, you are telling Julia: "Look for this neighbor relative to where I am right now".

    How it works: Julia records the path as a relative jump.

    Portability: If you move the entire Thermodynamic Model folder to a USB drive or email it to a friend, the "jump" from the RxnCo folder to the BioMethEQK folder stays exactly the same. The project remains "self-healing".

If Relative Paths are confusing...

If the dots (../) are giving you a headache during the setup phase, you can use the absolute path to get it working on your machine right now.

Just keep in mind that before you send the final version to anyone else, you might want to switch them to relative paths so they don't run into errors.

Gemini's Pro Tip: In the Pkg REPL, you can use Tab-completion with relative paths!

    Type: develop "../../

    Hit Tab.

    Julia will show you the folders available at that level. This takes the guesswork out of how many ../ you actually need.

