Thermodynamic Model: CO₂ Hydrogenation to Methanol

This directory contains the thermodynamic modeling engine for the Biomethanol Plant Design project.
🏗 System Architecture

This model has been refactored from standalone scripts into a modular package ecosystem.

    Core Logic: Individual engineering calculations (Equilibrium, Fugacity, Kinetics) are isolated into their own Julia packages located in Modules/.

    Central Interface: The ThermodynamicModel package acts as the central "brain." It aggregates all sub-modules and re-exports their functionality.

    User Experience: By simply running using ThermodynamicModel, you gain access to the entire suite of physics and solver functions without managing individual file inclusions.

📚 Function Documentation

Once you load the environment, the following functions are directly available for plant simulation:
1. Reactor Solver

rxnco(T, P, phi, guess, feed_co2, feed_h2) The primary solver that determines the extent of reaction based on operating conditions.

    Inputs:

        T: Temperature (Kelvin)

        P: Pressure (bar)

        phi: Fugacity coefficient vector (calculated via fugacity)

        guess: Initial guess for reaction extents (Vector [e1, e2])

        feed_co2 / feed_h2: Molar flow rates of feed components.

    Returns: An NLsolve object containing the converged reaction coordinates (.zero) and convergence status (converged).

2. Thermodynamics (SRK EOS)

fugacity(T, P) Calculates the non-ideality of the gas phase using the Soave-Redlich-Kwong (SRK) Equation of State.

    Inputs: T (K), P (bar)

    Returns: A vector of fugacity coefficients (ϕ) for the components in the mixture.

3. Performance Metrics

These functions take the converged reaction coordinates (roots) and calculate key plant performance indicators.

    yieldF(roots): Calculates the Methanol Yield (g_methanol / g_catalyst or % theoretical, depending on specific module definition).

    selectivityF(roots): Calculates Methanol Selectivity relative to side products (e.g., CO).

    conv(roots): Calculates the Single-pass Conversion of CO₂.

(Note: roots is the vector accessed via sol.zero from the solver output).
