see if you can make sense of this

This is extremely cleanly structured. You have successfully implemented a 3-Tier Architecture for your unit operation design. This is professional-grade software engineering applied to chemical engineering.

Here is the breakdown of why this works and what it is physically doing:
1. The Architecture (It makes perfect sense)

You have separated your concerns into three distinct layers:

    Tier 1: The Data Layer (StripperBasis.jl)

        This is your "Single Source of Truth."

        It holds the hardcoded PFD values (Flows, Henry's Constant).

        Benefit: If you change the Feed Flow in the simulation, you only update this one file, and the entire design recalculates automatically.

    Tier 2: The Logic Layer (StripperXYEQ.jl)

        This contains the actual Mass Transfer physics.

        It converts your raw data into Mole Ratios (X and Y).

        Why this is smart: You are using Mole Ratios (X=x/(1−x)) instead of Mole Fractions. In a stripper, the total liquid flow (L) decreases as CO₂ leaves. Using Solute-Free Ratios (X) allows you to use straight operating lines even when the flow changes. This is the correct textbook approach.

    Tier 3: The Interface Layer (StripperMB.jl & McCabe_Thiele.jl)

        StripperMB bundles everything together.

        McCabe_Thiele is just a script that "turns the key" and draws the picture.

2. The Physics Verification

I checked your math in calculate_op_line_points against standard mass transfer principles:

    Stripper vs. Absorber:

        Equilibrium Slope (m) ≈160/45=3.55.

        Operating Slope (L′/V′) ≈926/1320=0.70.

        Since Slope(Eq) > Slope(Op), the Equilibrium curve will be steep, and your Operating Line will lie below it.

        Result: This correctly simulates Stripping (driving force is from Liquid to Gas). If the operating line were above, it would be an absorber.

3. One Visual Check for You

When you run McCabe_Thiele.jl, your plot should look like this:

    Black Line (Eq): Steep, going up from (0,0).

    Blue Line (Op): Flatter, starting at Xbottom​ (near 0) and ending at Xfeed​ (higher value).

    The Gap: The vertical distance between the Blue and Black lines represents your Driving Force.

4. How to link this to your Main Project

To make this StripperMB usable by your main ThermodynamicModel (or just accessible alongside it), you need to register these dependencies.

Since StripperMB depends on StripperBasis and StripperXYEQ, and those look like separate local packages in your Project.toml, you need to ensure StripperMB knows where they are.

Run this in the Pkg REPL (]) while inside the StripperMB folder:
Julia

(StripperMB) pkg> develop "../StripperBasis"  # (Adjust path to where you put it)
(StripperMB) pkg> develop "../StripperXYEQ"

Once that is linked, you can run your McCabe_Thiele.jl script , and it will automatically pull the constants from StripperBasis , calculate the ratios in StripperXYEQ, and generate the design.

