Here is the README.md for your StripperMB module. It follows the same clean, professional structure as the previous one, highlighting the tiered architecture and usage instructions.

You can save this file inside the Thermodynamic Model/Modules/Separation/StripperMB folder.
StripperMB: Methanol Stripper Design Module

This directory contains the design and simulation engine for the CO₂ Stripping Column.

Unlike the reaction models, this module is built to generate visual design data using the McCabe-Thiele Method. It uses a tiered architecture to separate raw PFD data from the mass transfer physics.
🏗 System Architecture

The module is split into three layers to ensure data consistency across the plant design:

    StripperBasis (Data Layer):

        Contains the "Single Source of Truth" for process flows (Methanol/Water rates, H2​ gas feed) and physical constants (Henry's Law constant, Pressure).

        Benefit: Changing a flow rate here automatically updates the entire design.

    StripperXYEQ (Logic Layer):

        Handles the mass transfer physics.

        Converts raw flows into Mole Ratios (X,Y) to ensure linear operating lines even with changing total flow rates.

        Calculates the coordinates for the Operating Line and Equilibrium Curve.

    StripperMB (Interface):

        The central package that bundles the basis and logic together for the user.

📦 Available Functions

By running using StripperMB, you gain access to the following functions to generate your design diagrams.
                        
1. Design Logic (StripperXYEQ)

These are the core functions used to generate the lines for the McCabe-Thiele plot.

    calculate_op_line_points(target_removal)

        Purpose: Calculates the start and end coordinates of the Operating Line for a specific performance target.

        Input: target_removal (Float, e.g., 0.995 for 99.5% removal).

        Returns: An OpLineCoords object containing:

            X_top / Y_top: Coordinates at the column top (Rich Gas / Feed Liquid).

            X_btm / Y_btm: Coordinates at the column bottom (Lean Gas / Lean Liquid).

    calculate_equilibrium_curve(num_points)

        Purpose: Generates the Equilibrium Curve based on Henry's Law (m=H/P).

        Input: num_points (Integer, default 100).

        Returns: Two arrays (X_ratios, Y_ratios) ready for plotting.

2. Process Data Accessors (StripperBasis)

Direct access to the underlying PFD values used in the calculation.

    calculate_L0(): Returns total liquid molar flow rate entering the stripper.

    calculate_xA0(): Returns the mole fraction of solute (CO2​) in the feed.

    calculate_L_prime(): Returns the solute-free liquid flow (Methanol + Water).

    calculate_V_prime(): Returns the solute-free gas flow (H2​ carrier).

    calculate_equilibrium_m(): Returns the calculated Henry's Law constant (m) at operating pressure.

    How to Run the Design

    1. Initial Setup

      Since this module requires visualization tools (PyPlot), you must instantiate the environment first.

      Open this folder in your terminal.

      Enter the Package Manager (]).

      Run:
          (v1.x) pkg> activate .
          (StripperMB) pkg> instantiate  

   2. Generating the Diagram

      We have provided a visualization script, McCabe_Thiele.jl, that automatically calculates the curves and generates the plot.

      To run it:

                julia> include("McCabe_Thiele.jl")

      Output: A window will generate showing the Equilibrium Curve (Black) and Operating Line (Blue), along with the specific coordinates in the console.                       

      3. Editing the Design Target

      To verify if the stripper can handle different removal requirements (e.g., 95% vs 99.9%), open McCabe_Thiele.jl and edit the target variable at the top:  

      # ==========================================
      # 1. SETUP
      # ==========================================
      target = 0.995  # <--- CHANGE THIS (e.g., to 0.95 or 0.999)   

       Rerunning the script will instantly update the Operating Line slope and coordinates to reflect the new design requirement.                        
